  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 3,
    "lesson_title": "Frequency Response $H(j\\omega)$",
    "lesson_core_move": "Interpret $H(j\\omega)$ as the frequency-indexed complex gain that an LTI system applies to a complex exponential input $e^{j\\omega t}$, producing a same-frequency output.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Frequency Response $H(j\\omega)$",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Frequency Response $H(j\\omega)$",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Frequency response is the system's response profile indexed by angular frequency. For each $\\omega$, $H(j\\omega)$ names the complex gain an LTI system applies at that frequency.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=e^{j\\omega_0 t}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use $x(t)=e^{j\\omega_0 t}$ as the probe input. Here $\\omega_0$ is the frequency being tested, so the notation asks only how the system responds at that one angular frequency.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=H(j\\omega_0)e^{j\\omega_0 t}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Read $H(j\\omega_0)$ as the complex gain at angular frequency $\\omega_0$. The output keeps the same exponential $e^{j\\omega_0 t}$; only the multiplier changes with $\\omega$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "H(j\\omega_0)=\\text{the complex gain at angular frequency }\\omega_0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "That is the meaning of frequency response: same frequency in, same frequency out, with the LTI system contributing a frequency-dependent complex multiplier.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Frequency Response $H(j\\omega)$",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Matching a Complex Exponential Input to $H(j\\omega_0)$",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "An LTI system is driven by $x(t)=e^{j6t}$. Identify the frequency-response sample used for this input and write the output in indexed-gain form.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The exponent tells us the probe frequency is $\\omega_0=6$. So the matching frequency-response sample is $H(j6)$, not a generic $H(j\\omega)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=e^{j6t}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For this probe input, the output stays at the same angular frequency and picks up the complex gain at that frequency.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=H(j6)e^{j6t}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the output is the same exponential scaled by $H(j6)$. The only index we had to read from the input was the $6$ inside the exponent.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Frequency Response $H(j\\omega)$",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Interpreting $H(j\\omega_0)$ as a Complex Gain",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Suppose an LTI system has $H(j2)=3e^{-j\\pi/6}$. Interpret what this value means for the response to $e^{j2t}$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the index $2$: use the sample $H(j2)$, not a different frequency. Read that sample as the complex multiplier at angular frequency $2$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "H(j2)=3e^{-j\\pi/6}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the response to the matching input $e^{j2t}$ is the same input scaled by that gain.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=H(j2)e^{j2t}=3e^{-j\\pi/6}e^{j2t}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The output stays at frequency $2$. The only change is the complex gain factor.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Frequency Response $H(j\\omega)$",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Writing the Same-Frequency Output Using $H(j\\omega_0)$",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "For $x(t)=e^{j3t}$ and $H(j3)=1-j$, write the output expression using the same input frequency.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Match the input frequency to the response sample: the probe is $e^{j3t}$, so the indexed gain is $H(j3)$. Use that gain as a multiplier, not as a new signal.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=e^{j3t}, \\quad H(j3)=1-j",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Write the output by multiplying the input by the indexed gain and keep the exponent unchanged.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=H(j3)e^{j3t}=(1-j)e^{j3t}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The frequency stays at $3$; only the complex factor changes, so the gain belongs in front of the same exponential.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Frequency Response $H(j\\omega)$",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Comparing Complex Gains at Different Frequencies",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "An LTI system has $H(j1)=2$ and $H(j4)=1-j$. Compare the response gains applied to $e^{jt}$ and $e^{j4t}$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Read the two frequencies separately: $e^{jt}$ uses $H(j1)$, and $e^{j4t}$ uses $H(j4)$. Do not reuse one gain for both inputs.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "H(j1)=2, \\quad H(j4)=1-j",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now pair each input with its own indexed gain and keep the output frequency unchanged.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y_1(t)=H(j1)e^{jt}=2e^{jt}, \\quad y_4(t)=H(j4)e^{j4t}=(1-j)e^{j4t}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The comparison is the gain values themselves: the multiplier is $2$ at frequency $1$ and $1-j$ at frequency $4$. The response changes with frequency.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
